<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|A Constructive Introduction to Quantum Field
  Theory>|<doc-subtitle|Lecture 2: The Gaussian free
  field>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|M.
  Gubinelli>|<author-affiliation|Mathematical Institute, University of
  Oxford>>>|<doc-date|SISSA, October 2026>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    We study the first example of a Euclidean field theory: the Gaussian free
    field (GFF) on the torus <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>> and on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>. We construct it, prove reflection positivity
    and recover the free relativistic field, discuss the Euclidean
    Källén\ULehmann representation, and define its local (Wick) operators. We
    prove that the Dyson\USchwinger equation characterises the GFF among
    probability measures on distributions, and that the GFF is the stationary
    one-time (resp. one-point) marginal of the parabolic (resp. elliptic)
    stochastic quantisation equation. Exercises are collected at the end.
    These notes follow the slides of Lecture 2.
  </abstract>>

