  <section*|Introduction>

  In Lecture 1 we saw that, after the passage to imaginary time, a
  relativistic quantum field is encoded in a probability measure on
  distributions satisfying the Osterwalder\USchrader axioms, formally
  <math|\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<propto\>exp
  <around|(|-<big|int><around|[|<frac|1|2>\|\<nabla\>*\<varphi\>\|<rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>*m<rsup|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>+V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>|)>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>
  (Eq.<nbsp><hlink|(2.13)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:path-integral>). In this
  lecture we treat its Gaussian part rigorously. Conventions are those of
  Lecture 1. Sections, equations and exercises keep the numbering of the
  notes, so that Ÿ1\U2 refer to the notes of Lecture 1.

  <assign|section-nr|2><section|The Gaussian free field><label|sec:gff>

  <subsection|Definition and construction>

  Throughout, <math|\<cal-M\>> is either the torus
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>=<around|(|\<bbb-R\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|d>>
  or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, <math|d\<geq\>1>. We write
  <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> for
  <math|C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>>, resp. the
  Schwartz space, and <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> for
  its dual. We equip <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with
  the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra generated by the maps
  <math|\<varphi\>\<mapsto\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>; it coincides with the
  Borel <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra<nbsp><cite|GV64|Bog98>. Test functions are
  real unless stated otherwise. Let <math|m\<gtr\>0> and

  <\equation>
    A\<assign\>m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>,<space|2em>C\<assign\>A<rsup|-1>.
  </equation>

  <math|C> is a positive bounded operator on
  <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C|\<\|\|\>>\<leq\>m<rsup|-2>>. Both <math|A> and
  <math|C> are continuous bijections of <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
  being Fourier multipliers by <math|<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|\<pm\>1>>.

  <\definition>
    <label|def:gff>The <em|(massive) Gaussian free field> on <math|\<cal-M\>>
    is the centred Gaussian probability measure <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>
    on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with covariance
    <math|C>:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:gff-cov><big|int>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>=<big|iint>f<around|(|x|)>*G*<around|(|x,y|)>*g<around|(|y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>y,
    </equation>

    equivalently <math|<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    for all <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>. Here <math|G> is
    the Green function of <math|A>. On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
    <math|G*<around|(|x,y|)>=G<rsub|m>*<around|(|x-y|)>> with
    <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><frac|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>k\<cdot\>x>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>>*<frac|\<mathd\>k|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|d>>>.
    On <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>>, <math|G*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>>G<rsub|m>*<around|(|x-y+n*L|)>>
    (method of images).
  </definition>

  Formally <math|\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>*`*`="*Z<rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,A*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>,
  the Gaussian part of <hlink|(2.13)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:path-integral>. The
  Green function satisfies (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:green>):
  <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<sim\><frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
  <frac|1|<around|\||x|\|>>> as <math|x\<to\>0> if <math|d=2>;
  <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<sim\><frac|\<Gamma\>*<around|(|d/2-1|)>|4*\<pi\><rsup|d/2>><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2-d>>
  if <math|d\<geq\>3>; <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-m<around|\||x|\|>>/<around|(|2*m|)>>
  if <math|d=1>; and <math|G<rsub|m>> decays like
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-<around|(|d-1|)>/2>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<around|\||x|\|>>>
  at infinity. Uniqueness of <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> is clear, since a
  probability measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> is
  determined by its characteristic functional.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|gff2d.png>|0.38par|||>
  <image|<tuple|<#>|green_functions.pdf>|0.58par|||><label|fig:gff>>|Left:
  a sample of the massive GFF on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> (<math|m=4>,
  Fourier cutoff <math|512<rsup|2>>, zero mode removed). It is not a
  function: refining the cutoff adds fluctuations at all scales. Right: the
  Green function <math|G<rsub|m>> in <math|d=1,2,3>: bounded, logarithmic and
  <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-1>> singularities at the origin, and
  exponential decay at infinity.>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Existence and regularity><label|thm:existence> (a) Let
    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>>, let
    <math|<around|(|e<rsub|n>|)><rsub|n\<geq\>0>> be a real orthonormal basis
    of <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>> of
    eigenfunctions of <math|-\<Delta\>>, <math|-\<Delta\>*e<rsub|n>=\<kappa\><rsub|n>*e<rsub|n>>,
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n>\<assign\>m<rsup|2>+\<kappa\><rsub|n>>, and let
    <math|<around|(|g<rsub|n>|)>> be i.i.d. <math|N<around|(|0,1|)>>. For
    every <math|s\<gtr\><frac|d|2>-1> the series

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:series>\<varphi\>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geq\>0>\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1/2>*g<rsub|n>*<space|0.17em>e<rsub|n>
    </equation>

    converges a.s. and in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-P\>;H<rsup|-s><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>|)>>,
    and its law is <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>. In particular
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|H<rsup|-s>|)>=1> for
    <math|s\<gtr\><frac|d|2>-1>. (b) On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> exists by the Bochner\UMinlos theorem.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (a) With <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|-s>><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|n><around|(|1+\<kappa\><rsub|n>|)><rsup|-s>*<around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|n>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>><rsup|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><rsub|H<rsup|-s>>=<big|sum><rsub|n><frac|1|<around|(|1+\<kappa\><rsub|n>|)><rsup|s>*\<lambda\><rsub|n>>\<asymp\><big|sum><rsub|k\<in\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>><around|(|1+<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-s-1>\<less\>\<infty\>\<Longleftrightarrow\>2*s+2\<gtr\>d.
    </equation*>

    The partial sums form an <math|L<rsup|2>>-bounded martingale with values
    in the Hilbert space <math|H<rsup|-s>>, so they converge a.s. and in
    <math|L<rsup|2>>. For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n>\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1/2>*g<rsub|n>*<around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|n>,f|\<rangle\>>>
    is centred Gaussian with variance <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1>*<around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|n>,f|\<rangle\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>.

    (b) Bochner\UMinlos<nbsp><cite|GV64|GJ87>: if
    <math|\<Psi\>:\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>\<to\>\<bbb-C\>> is
    continuous, positive definite and <math|\<Psi\><around|(|0|)>=1>, there
    is a unique probability measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>
    with characteristic functional <math|\<Psi\>>. For
    <math|\<Psi\><around|(|f|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>,
    continuity follows from <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>\<leq\>m<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>><rsup|2>>.
    For positive definiteness, write <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|f<rsub|j>-f<rsub|k>|)>=a<rsub|j>*a<rsub|k>\<mathe\><rsup|Q<rsub|j*k>>>
    with <math|a<rsub|j>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*Q<rsub|j*j>>> and
    <math|Q<rsub|j*k>=<around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|j>,C*f<rsub|k>|\<rangle\>>>
    positive semidefinite. Then <math|<around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|Q<rsub|j*k>>|)>>
    is positive semidefinite by the Schur product theorem.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    The Cameron\UMartin space of <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> is
    <math|H<rsup|1><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with norm
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,A*h|\<rangle\>><rsup|1/2>>: the fields of
    finite action. It has measure zero, and typical samples are much rougher
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:regularity>). In <math|d=1> the GFF on
    <math|\<bbb-R\>> is the stationary Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process. In
    <math|d=2> it lies in <math|H<rsup|-\<kappa\>>> for every
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> but is not a function. In <math|d=3> it lies in
    <math|H<rsup|-1/2-\<kappa\>>>. On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> the samples
    live in weighted Sobolev spaces.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Reflection positivity and the free field>

  Let <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-R\>\<times\>N> with <math|N=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d-1>>
  or <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d-1><rsub|L>>, points
  <math|<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>,
  <math|\<Theta\>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>=<around|(|-\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>.
  Then <math|A=-\<partial\><rsub|\<tau\>><rsup|2>+B> with
  <math|B\<assign\>m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|N>> on
  <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|N|)>>, and we set
  <math|\<omega\>\<assign\>B<rsup|1/2>\<geq\>m>.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:RP>(a) As an operator-valued kernel in the time variables,
    <math|C*<around|(|\<tau\>,\<tau\><rprime|'>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||\<tau\>-\<tau\><rprime|'>|\|>*\<omega\>><around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    (b) For <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with
    <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\><around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:RP><around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f,C*f|\<rangle\>>=<around*|\||<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1/2><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*\<omega\>>f(\<tau\>,\<cdot\><around*|)|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\>|\|><rsup|2><rsub|L<rsup|2><around|(|N|)>>\<geq\>0.|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation>

    (c) <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> satisfies
    (OS0)\U(OS4).
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    (a) follows from the partial Fourier transform in <math|\<tau\>>, the
    spectral theorem for <math|B>, and the scalar identity
    <math|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>k*t><around|(|k<rsup|2>+w<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<frac|\<mathd\>k|2*\<pi\>>=\<mathe\><rsup|-w<around|\||t|\|>>/<around|(|2*w|)>>,
    <math|w\<gtr\>0>. (b) Since <math|\<Theta\>*f> is supported in
    <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<less\>0|}>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<big|iint><rsub|\<tau\>\<less\>0\<less\>\<tau\><rprime|'>><around|\<langle\>|f*<around|(|-\<tau\>|)>,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|\<tau\><rprime|'>-\<tau\>|)>*\<omega\>><around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>*f<around|(|\<tau\><rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\><rprime|'>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><space|-0.17em><space|-0.17em><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\<langle\>|\<mathe\><rsup|-s*\<omega\>>f<around|(|s|)>,<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\><rprime|'>*\<omega\>>f<around|(|\<tau\><rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<tau\><rprime|'>,>>>>
    </align*>

    which is <eqref|eq:RP>. (c) (OS0)\U(OS2) and (OS4) follow from the
    explicit form of <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f|)>> and the decay of
    <math|G<rsub|m>>. For (OS3), <math|C> commutes with <math|\<Theta\>>, so
    <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^>*<around|(|f<rsub|i>-\<Theta\>*f<rsub|j>|)>=a<rsub|i>*a<rsub|j>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f<rsub|i>,C*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>>>
    with <math|a<rsub|i>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,C*f<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>>.
    The matrix <math|<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f<rsub|i>,C*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>|)><rsub|i*j>>
    is positive semidefinite by (b), hence so is its entrywise exponential
    (Schur).
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
