    \ \ \\fill[MidnightBlue!8] (-3,0) rectangle (3,2.2);

    \ \ \\draw[-\>] (-3.2,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {\\small $\\mathbf{x}\\in
    N$};

    \ \ \\draw[-\>] (0,-2.3) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {\\small $\\tau$};

    \ \ \\draw[thick,dashed,BrickRed] (-3.2,0) -- (3.2,0);

    \ \ \\fill[MidnightBlue!60] (1.2,1.2) ellipse (0.7 and 0.35);

    \ \ \\node at (1.2,1.75) {\\small $\\operatorname{supp} f$};

    \ \ \\fill[MidnightBlue!25] (1.2,-1.2) ellipse (0.7 and 0.35);

    \ \ \\node at (1.2,-1.75) {\\small $\\operatorname{supp}\\Theta f$};

    \ \ \\draw[\<-\>,gray] (2.1,1.2) -- (2.1,-1.2) node[midway,right]
    {\\small $\\Theta$};

    \ \ \\node[BrickRed,anchor=east] at (-0.2,0.25) {\\small $\\tau=0$};

    \ \ \\node at (-1.7,1.3) {\\small $\\{\\tau\>0\\}$};

    \\end{tikzpicture}

    \\end{document}
  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>><label|fig:RP>>|Reflection
  positivity (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:RP>). For <math|f> supported
  in <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>> the covariance between <math|f> and
  its mirror image factorises through the time-zero plane:
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f,C*f|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1/2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<tau\>*\<omega\>>f<around|(|\<tau\>|)>\<mathd\>\<tau\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geq\>0>.>

  By the OS reconstruction theorem the GFF yields a Wightman theory, the free
  field of mass <math|m> of Section<nbsp><hlink|1.3|cqft_notes_L1.tm#sec:distrib>.
  Concretely, the reconstructed Hilbert space is the Fock space over
  <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|N|)>> with <math|H=\<mathd\>\<Gamma\><around|(|\<omega\>|)>>
  (Nelson's free Markov field<nbsp><cite|Nel73a|Nel73b>). The time-zero
  restriction of the GFF is Gaussian with covariance
  <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>>, the ground state measure of the
  free Hamiltonian in the Schrödinger representation
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:slices>). The GFF is also the continuum limit
  of the lattice model <hlink|(2.12)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:spin-system> with
  <math|V=0> (for <math|d=2>; in general <math|d> see
  Exercise<nbsp><hlink|2.3|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:lattice-action> and
  Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:lattice-gff>).

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Nelson's Markov property><label|rem:markov> For
    <math|U\<subset\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> open let <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|U>> be
    the <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra generated by the
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>> with <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\>U>,
    and for <math|K> closed let <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>=<big|cap><rsub|U\<supset\>K>\<Sigma\><rsub|U>>
    (the <em|germ> <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra of <math|K>). A random field is
    <em|Markov> for an open set <math|\<Lambda\>> if
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<wide|\<Lambda\>|\<bar\>>>> and
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|\<Lambda\><rsup|c>>> are conditionally independent
    given <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>>>: the field inside and
    the field outside interact only through the field near the boundary.

    (i) <em|The GFF is Markov for every open
    <math|\<Lambda\>>><nbsp><cite|Nel73a>. For the GFF,
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>> is generated by the
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>> with <math|g\<in\>H<rsup|-1>> supported
    in <math|K><nbsp><cite|Nel73a>. In Gaussian terms, conditional
    expectations are then orthogonal projections in the Hilbert space of the
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>, with
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>>:
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|\<Lambda\><rsup|c>>|]>=\<varphi\>*<around|(|\<Pi\>*f|)>>,
    where <math|\<Pi\>> projects <math|H<rsup|-1>> onto the distributions
    supported in <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|c>>. Let
    <math|<math-up|supp>f\<subset\>\<Lambda\>> and <math|u=C*\<Pi\>*f>.
    Orthogonality gives <math|u=C*f> on the interior of
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|c>>, hence <math|A*u=f=0> there, while
    <math|A*u=\<Pi\>*f=0> in <math|\<Lambda\>>. So <math|\<Pi\>*f=A*u> is
    supported on <math|\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>>: this is where the
    <em|locality> of <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>> enters. Equivalently, given
    the outside, the field in <math|\<Lambda\>> is the <math|A>-harmonic
    extension of its boundary values plus an independent GFF with Dirichlet
    conditions on <math|\<partial\>*\<Lambda\>> (the domain Markov property
    of<nbsp><cite|She07>). The generalised free fields of the two-mass
    example below are not Markov, because their <math|C<rsup|-1>> is
    nonlocal.

    (ii) <em|For a <math|\<Theta\>>-invariant field, Markov with a sharp
    time-zero <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra implies reflection positivity.> Take
    <math|\<Lambda\>=<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>> and let
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>> be the germ <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra of
    <math|<around|{|\<tau\>=0|}>>. For the GFF it is generated by the
    time-zero field (<em|sharp> Markov property), so <math|\<Theta\>> acts
    trivially on it. If <math|F> is <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<around|{|\<tau\>\<geq\>0|}>>>-measurable,
    then <math|\<Theta\>*F> is <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<around|{|\<tau\>\<leq\>0|}>>>-measurable
    and <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Theta\>*F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>>,
    so the Markov property gives

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|<wide|\<Theta\>*F|\<bar\>>*<space|0.17em>F|]>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<wide|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Theta\>*F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>|\<bar\>><space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><around|[|F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<around|\||\<bbb-E\><around|[|F<space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>|\|><rsup|2>|]>\<geq\>0.
    </equation*>

    Sharpness is essential: the fourth-order field with covariance
    <math|P<rsup|-1>> in the discussion of positivity versus locality below
    is Markov but not reflection positive, because its
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>> also contains
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<tau\>>*\<varphi\>>, which <math|\<Theta\>>
    reverses. Moreover <math|P<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\>[<space|0.17em>U<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|\||<space|0.17em>\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|]>>,
    with <math|U<rsub|t>> the time translation, is a semigroup on
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|0>|)>>: by the Markov property
    for <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>t|}>>,
    <math|P<rsub|t>*P<rsub|s>=P<rsub|t+s>>. It is self-adjoint (by
    <math|\<Theta\>>-invariance), positive
    (<math|P<rsub|t>=P<rsub|t/2><rsup|2>>), contractive and strongly
    continuous, so <math|P<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-t*H>>, and the physical
    Hilbert space is <math|L<rsup|2>> of the time-zero field, i.e. the
    Schrödinger representation. This is Nelson's reconstruction of quantum
    fields from Euclidean invariant Markov fields<nbsp><cite|Nel73b>, which
    preceded the Osterwalder\USchrader axioms, and it underlies the
    Feynman\UKac\UNelson formula (Proposition<nbsp><hlink|2.3|cqft_notes_L1.tm#prop:FK>).

    (iii) <em|Relevance.> A local perturbation
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|\<Lambda\>>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>>
    of <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> factorises over regions and preserves the Markov
    property. This makes the <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> measures
    in finite volume Markov fields, and it gives the classical statistical
    mechanics picture of<nbsp><cite|GRS75>: conditional distributions given
    the outside, DLR equations, correlation inequalities. The Markov property
    is not stable under weak limits, however, and is hard to establish
    directly for infinite-volume or renormalised theories. Osterwalder and
    Schrader replaced it by reflection positivity, which neither implies nor
    is implied by the Markov property (generalised free fields are reflection
    positive but not Markov, the <math|P<rsup|-1>> field is Markov but not
    reflection positive), but is preserved under weak limits.
    See<nbsp><cite|Sim74|GJ87|Gue06> for more.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Symanzik, Brownian paths and loop soups><label|rem:symanzik> The
    Euclidean programme goes back to Symanzik<nbsp><cite|Sym66|Sym69>, who
    proposed to study QFT through its Schwinger functions, viewed as the
    moments of a classical (formally, Markov) random field, before Nelson and
    Osterwalder\USchrader made this rigorous. In<nbsp><cite|Sym69> he also
    introduced a representation of the field in terms of Brownian paths.
    Since

    <\equation*>
      C*<around|(|x,y|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsup|2>*t><space|0.17em>p<rsub|t><around|(|x,y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t,
    </equation*>

    with <math|p<rsub|t>> the heat kernel of <math|\<Delta\>>, the two-point
    function of the GFF is the Green function of a Brownian motion (with
    generator <math|\<Delta\>>) killed at rate <math|m<rsup|2>>. For
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsub|m>>,
    the correlations become averages over a gas of Brownian paths and loops
    with an interaction that depends on their local times. For
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>> the interaction penalises intersections. On the
    lattice this is the random walk representation of Brydges, Fröhlich and
    Spencer<nbsp><cite|BFS82>, the basis of one of the proofs of triviality
    of <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4><rsub|d>> for <math|d\<gtr\>4>. Heuristically,
    the interaction becomes ineffective in the continuum for <math|d\<geq\>4>
    because two independent Brownian paths do not intersect there.

    The modern continuum version is the <em|Brownian loop soup> of Lawler and
    Werner<nbsp><cite|LW04>, a Poisson process of unrooted Brownian loops
    with intensity <math|\<alpha\>> times the loop measure. On a finite graph
    the occupation field <math|L> of the random-walk loop soup satisfies
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,L|\<rangle\>>>|]>=det
    <around|(|1+C*\<chi\>|)><rsup|-\<alpha\>>>, while
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<chi\>,\<varphi\><rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>>|]>=det
    <around|(|1+C*\<chi\>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. So for <math|\<alpha\>=<frac|1|2>>,
    <math|L> has the law of <math|<frac|1|2>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>>: this is
    <em|Le Jan's isomorphism><nbsp><cite|LJ10|LJ11>, a loop version of
    Dynkin's isomorphism<nbsp><cite|Dyn84>. In the continuum, in
    <math|d=2,3>, the renormalised occupation field of the soup corresponds
    to <math|<frac|1|2>:\<varphi\><rsup|2>>:, the Wick square of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wick>. Lupu<nbsp><cite|Lup16> showed that on
    the cable graph the clusters of the <math|\<alpha\>=<frac|1|2>> soup are
    exactly the sign clusters of the GFF, which is recovered from the soup by
    tossing independent signs. Qian and Werner<nbsp><cite|QW19> decomposed
    the clusters of the two-dimensional continuum soup, and
    Werner<nbsp><cite|Wer16> proved a spatial Markov property for loop soups
    that parallels Nelson's Markov property of the GFF. For a survey
    see<nbsp><cite|WP21>.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Euclidean Källén\ULehmann representation><label|rem:KL-euclid> The
    computation in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:RP>(a) says that
    <math|<big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>\|\<tau\>\|+\<mathi\><math-bf|p>\<cdot\><math-bf|x>>=G<rsub|M>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>>:
    the Wick rotation of the free two-point function
    <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|M|)>>> is the Green function
    <math|G<rsub|M>> of <math|M<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>>. Rotating
    <hlink|(1.4)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:KL> term by term, the Schwinger
    two-point function of any theory satisfying the Wightman (or OS) axioms
    is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:KL-euclid>S<rsub|2><around|(|x|)>=v<rsup|2>+<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>x\<neq\>0,<space|2em><text|i.e.><space|2em><wide|S|^><rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|k|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>><frac|\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+M<rsup|2>>,
    </equation>

    a Stieltjes transform in <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>>. (The Fourier
    form holds up to contact terms, i.e. a polynomial in
    <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>> invisible at <math|x\<neq\>0>, and
    provided <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|1+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<less\>\<infty\>>;
    otherwise subtractions are needed.) The GFF is the case
    <math|\<rho\>=\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>. Three consequences follow.

    (i) <em|Short distances.> <math|G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>> is decreasing in
    <math|M>, and for <math|d\<geq\>2> we have
    <math|G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>/G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<to\>1> as
    <math|x\<to\>0>, for every <math|M>. Hence, for every <math|K>,
    <math|S<rsup|T><rsub|2><around|(|x|)>\<geq\>\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,K<rsup|2>|]>|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|K><around|(|x|)>>,
    and, if <math|\<rho\>\<neq\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      liminf<rsub|x\<to\>0> <frac|S<rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|x|)>|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>>\<geq\>\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>\<in\><around|(|0,\<infty\>|]>.
    </equation*>

    The two-point function of any nontrivial theory is at least as singular
    as the free one. In particular a scalar field cannot have scaling
    dimension below the free value <math|<frac|d-2|2>> (the <em|unitarity
    bound>, Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:KL-euclid>).

    (ii) <em|Long distances.> If <math|<math-up|supp>\<rho\>\<subset\><around|[|m<rsub|0><rsup|2>,\<infty\>|)>>,
    then <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|x|)>> decays at least like
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsub|0><around|\||x|\|>>>, up to subexponential
    factors, and exactly at this rate if <math|m<rsub|0><rsup|2>=inf
    <math-up|supp>\<rho\>>. Conversely, exponential decay at rate
    <math|m<rsub|0>> forces <math|<math-up|supp>\<rho\>\<subset\><around|[|m<rsub|0><rsup|2>,\<infty\>|)>>.
    So a gap in the spectral measure of <math|\<varphi\>> is the same as
    exponential decay of <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|T>>; a mass gap of <math|H>
    corresponds to exponential clustering of all truncated correlations
    (compare Exercise<nbsp><hlink|2.6|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:vacuum>).

    (iii) <em|Generalised free fields.> For any positive measure
    <math|\<rho\>> on <math|<around|(|0,\<infty\>|)>> with
    <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|1+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    and <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*M<rsup|-2>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    the Gaussian measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>
    with covariance <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>=<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|M<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>>
    satisfies the OS axioms: <math|<wide|C|^><rsub|\<rho\>>\<leq\><wide|C|^><rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|0|)>=<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*M<rsup|-2>>
    is bounded, which gives (OS1) with <math|p=2>, and reflection positivity
    follows from Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:RP> for each mass
    <math|M\<gtr\>0> and <math|\<rho\>\<geq\>0>. (If only the first condition
    holds, the measure may still exist and satisfy the other axioms, but
    (OS1) in the form of Lecture 1 can fail: for the massless field in
    <math|d\<geq\>3>, <math|f=\<mathi\>g> with <math|g\<geq\>0> supported in
    a ball of radius <math|R> gives <math|<around|\||<wide|\<mu\>|^><around|(|f|)>|\|>=\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|g,C*g|\<rangle\>>>>
    with <math|<around|\<langle\>|g,C*g|\<rangle\>>\<sim\>R<rsup|d+2>>.) It
    reconstructs the generalised free field of
    Exercise<nbsp><hlink|1.6|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:gen-free>. Its
    Dyson\USchwinger equation <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F*\<varphi\>*<around|(|C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>*h|)>|]>>
    still characterises it: under mild conditions on <math|\<rho\>> (e.g.
    <math|\<rho\>> finite with a mass gap), <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>> is a
    bijection of <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>|)>>, which is all
    the proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:DS-unique> uses. However
    <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>> is <em|nonlocal> unless <math|\<rho\>> is
    a single atom, as the following example shows.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|Example: two masses.> Let <math|0\<less\>m<rsub|1>\<less\>m<rsub|2>>,
  <math|c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|2>\<gtr\>0>, and

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<rho\>>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  <em|Euclidean theory.> The Gaussian measure <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\>>>
  with covariance <math|C<rsub|\<rho\>>> is the law of
  <math|c<rsub|1>*\<varphi\><rsub|1>+c<rsub|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|2>>, where
  <math|\<varphi\><rsub|1>,\<varphi\><rsub|2>> are independent GFFs on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> of masses <math|m<rsub|1>,m<rsub|2>>. It satisfies
  (OS0)\U(OS4); reflection positivity follows from
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:RP> applied to each term.

  <em|The Minkowski theory.> Since a Wightman theory is determined up to
  unitary equivalence by its Wightman functions, which are in turn determined
  by the Schwinger functions, <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\>>> reconstructs to
  exactly the following theory. Let <math|\<frak-h\><rsub|m>=L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d-1>,\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|m>|)>>
  be the one-particle space of mass <math|m> and spin <math|0>, carrying the
  irreducible positive-energy representation <math|u<rsub|m>> of
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsup|\<uparrow\>><rsub|+>>. Then

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-H\>=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|1>>\<oplus\>\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|2>>|)>\<simeq\>\<Gamma\><around|(|\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|1>>|)>\<otimes\>\<Gamma\><around|(|\<frak-h\><rsub|m<rsub|2>>|)>,<space|2em>U=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|u<rsub|m<rsub|1>>\<oplus\>u<rsub|m<rsub|2>>|)>,<space|2em>\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|1>\<otimes\>\<Omega\><rsub|2>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    \<varphi\><around|(|x|)>=c<rsub|1>*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|1>><around|(|x|)>\<otimes\><with|math-font|Bbb|1>+c<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em><with|math-font|Bbb|1>\<otimes\>\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|2>><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|\<varphi\><rsub|m>> the free field
  <hlink|(1.2)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:free-field> of mass <math|m>. The Wightman
  functions are given by Wick's theorem with
  <math|W<rsub|2>=c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|m<rsub|1>|)>>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*W<rsub|2><rsup|<around|(|m<rsub|2>|)>>>,
  and all truncated functions of order <math|\<geq\>3> vanish. Physically,
  these are <em|two species of free scalar particles>, of masses
  <math|m<rsub|1>> and <math|m<rsub|2>>, both created from the vacuum by the
  same field: <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>=c<rsub|1>*a<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<wide|f|~>\|<rsub|H<rsup|+><rsub|m<rsub|1>>>|)>*\<Omega\>+c<rsub|2>*a<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<wide|f|~>\|<rsub|H<rsup|+><rsub|m<rsub|2>>>|)>*\<Omega\>>.
  They do not scatter. The mass spectrum consists of the two shells
  <math|m<rsub|1>>, <math|m<rsub|2>> and of the multi-particle continuum from
  <math|2*m<rsub|1>>. If <math|m<rsub|2>\<gtr\>2*m<rsub|1>>, the second shell
  lies inside the two-particle continuum, but the heavy particle is stable
  because there is no interaction. This is the generalised free field of
  Exercise<nbsp><hlink|1.6|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:gen-free>.

  <em|What is lost.> (1) There is no field equation of second order:
  <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0>,
  but <math|\<varphi\>> solves no Klein\UGordon equation. The time-zero
  fields <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>,
  <math|\<pi\>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>> satisfy
  <math|<around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<pi\>*<around|(|0,<math-bf|y>|)>|]>=\<mathi\><around|(|c<rsub|1><rsup|2>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|<math-bf|x>-<math-bf|y>|)>>,
  which is canonical iff <math|c<rsub|1><rsup|2>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>=1> (the
  sum rule of Exercise<nbsp><hlink|1.5|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:sum-rule>). Even
  when they are canonical, they are not a complete set of Cauchy data:
  <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,\<cdot\>|)>> depends on
  <math|c<rsub|1>*\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|1>>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>> and
  <math|c<rsub|2>*\<varphi\><rsub|m<rsub|2>>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>
  separately, not only on their sum. (2) In Euclidean terms, the DS equation
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F*\<varphi\>*<around|(|C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>*h|)>|]>>
  involves the nonlocal operator

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|\<rho\>><rsup|-1>=P*R<rsup|-1>,<space|2em>P\<assign\><around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>,<space|2em>R\<assign\>c<rsub|1><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>*<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Replacing <math|h> by <math|R*h> gives the equivalent local identity
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|R*h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F*\<varphi\>*<around|(|P*h|)>|]>>:
  a fourth-order Euclidean field equation whose contact term is itself
  differentiated, the Euclidean form of <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=0>.
  Correspondingly, <math|\<varphi\>> has no Markov property: for stationary
  Gaussian fields with a rational spectral density, such as
  <math|<wide|C|^><rsub|\<rho\>>=<wide|R|^>/<wide|P|^>>, the Markov property
  holds iff the inverse covariance is a differential operator, i.e. iff
  <math|1/<wide|C|^>> is a polynomial<nbsp><cite|Roz82>, which is not the
  case here. The pair <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|1>,\<varphi\><rsub|2>|)>>
  is Markov, but its sum is not: this is the Euclidean counterpart
  of<nbsp>(1).

  <em|Positivity versus locality.> Conversely, the local fourth-order action
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,P*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>
  defines a Markov Gaussian field with covariance

  <\equation*>
    P<rsup|-1>=<frac|<around|(|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>-<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>,<space|2em><text|i.e.><space|2em>\<rho\>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>-\<delta\><rsub|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-m<rsub|1><rsup|2>>,
  </equation*>

  with a <em|negative> weight at <math|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>>. By
  <eqref|eq:KL-euclid> and the uniqueness of the Stieltjes transform it is
  not reflection positive (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:two-masses>). It
  reconstructs at best a theory with an indefinite metric, in which the
  particle of mass <math|m<rsub|2>> is a ghost of negative
  norm<nbsp><cite|PU50|LW69>. More generally, call the Euclidean field
  equation <em|local> if <math|C<rsup|-1>> is a differential operator, i.e.
  <math|<wide|C|^>=1/p<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)>> with <math|p> a
  polynomial; local field equations give Markov fields, and for rational
  spectral densities the converse holds<nbsp><cite|Roz82>. The Fourier
  transform of a covariance satisfying the OS axioms is
  <math|q<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)>+<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  with <math|\<rho\>\<geq\>0> and <math|q> a polynomial coming from contact
  terms invisible to <math|\<rho\>>. If <math|deg p\<geq\>1>, then
  <math|q=0>, since <math|1/p<around|(|s|)>\<to\>0> and the Stieltjes term
  tends to <math|0> as <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>. Moreover
  <math|s*<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|s+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
  increases to <math|\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>> as
  <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>, while <math|s/p<around|(|s|)>\<to\>0> if <math|deg
  p\<geq\>2>; hence <math|\<rho\>=0>, a contradiction. If <math|deg p=0>,
  <math|<wide|C|^>> is constant. So a centred Gaussian measure satisfying the
  OS axioms has a local Euclidean field equation iff its covariance is
  <math|c*<around|(|M<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> with <math|M\<gtr\>0>,
  or a multiple of the identity (white noise, whose reconstructed Hilbert
  space is <math|\<bbb-C\>*\<Omega\>>). Note that it is the covariance, not
  <math|\<rho\>>, that matters: <math|a*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+c*<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>>
  with <math|a\<gtr\>0> is reflection positive and has
  <math|\<rho\>=c*\<delta\><rsub|m<rsup|2>>>, but its field equation is
  nonlocal. <em|Positivity and locality of the Euclidean field equation
  together single out the free field> (up to the trivial white noise);
  locality is also behind Nelson's Markov property of the
  GFF<nbsp><cite|Nel73a>.

  <paragraph|Is the nonlocal theory physical?> In Minkowski space the
  two-mass theory is a perfectly good one. It satisfies all the Wightman
  axioms, including locality (W3): <math|<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|y|)>|]>>
  is a sum of free-field commutators and vanishes at spacelike separation.
  The energy is positive and the vacuum unique. It is trivial (no
  interaction) and redundant, but not inconsistent. \PNonlocal\Q refers only
  to the Euclidean field equation for <math|\<varphi\>> alone, equivalently
  (for rational spectral densities, as here) to the absence of the Markov
  property. On the Minkowski side this means that <math|\<varphi\>> alone is
  not a complete set of local degrees of freedom. Its time-zero data do not
  determine its evolution, and there is no Lagrangian or Hamiltonian written
  in terms of <math|\<varphi\>> only. The cure is to enlarge the field
  content: the pair <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|1>,\<varphi\><rsub|2>|)>>
  has a local action and is Markov, and <math|\<varphi\>> is a linear
  combination of its components.

  Generalised free fields become genuinely unphysical when <math|\<rho\>> has
  a continuous part, i.e. (heuristically) infinitely many free species with
  masses in a bounded interval. With finitely many masses, <math|\<varphi\>>
  solves a hyperbolic equation of finite order, and the fields in an
  arbitrarily thin time slice generate all the observables: the
  <em|time-slice axiom>, or primitive causality<nbsp><cite-detail|Haa96|ŸII.1>.
  With a continuum of masses there is no dynamical law of finite order, and
  this property is not expected to hold. More decisively, the theory violates
  the phase-space requirements that express \Pfinitely many degrees of
  freedom per unit volume of phase space\Q, namely the compactness criterion
  of Haag and Swieca<nbsp><cite|HSw65> and its sharpening, the nuclearity
  condition of Buchholz and Wichmann<nbsp><cite|BW86>. For a system of free
  fields the nuclearity condition holds iff
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>n<rsub|i>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<beta\>*m<rsub|i>>\<less\>\<infty\>>
  for all <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>, where <math|n<rsub|i>> is the multiplicity
  of the mass <math|m<rsub|i>><nbsp><cite-detail|Haa96|ŸV.5>, and this fails
  when infinitely many species have masses in a bounded interval. Since the
  nuclearity index of a bounded region plays the role of its partition
  function, the theory has no sensible thermodynamics. This does not affect
  interacting theories, whose spectral measure also has a continuous part,
  the multi-particle continuum. There the continuum is produced by a
  <em|local> interaction, and the Euclidean field equation remains local,
  e.g. <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>[F(<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\>+4*\<lambda\>:\<varphi\><rsup|3>:<around|)|<around|(|h|)>|]>>
  for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>, although the inverse two-point function
  is nonlocal. Thus, for a single field: positive and nonlocal means an
  incomplete description, which is unphysical for continuous <math|\<rho\>>;
  local and not positive means ghosts; local, positive and with a nontrivial
  continuum in <math|\<rho\>> requires interactions.

  <subsection|Local operators: Wick products><label|sec:wick>

  Interactions <math|<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)>\<mathd\>x>
  and nonlinear field equations require local functions of
  <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>, but <math|\<varphi\>> is not a function
  for <math|d\<geq\>2>. We regularise, subtract, and remove the
  regularisation. The subtraction is encoded in Hermite polynomials with
  variance <math|c\<geq\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hermite>\<mathe\><rsup|t*a-c*t<rsup|2>/2>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geq\>0><frac|t<rsup|n>|n!>*H<rsub|n><around|(|a;c|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|2>=a<rsup|2>-c,<space|1em>H<rsub|3>=a<rsup|3>-3*c*a,<space|1em>H<rsub|4>=a<rsup|4>-6*c*a<rsup|2>+3*c<rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  <\lemma>
    <label|lem:hermite>If <math|<around|(|X,Y|)>> is a centred Gaussian
    vector with <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsup|2>=a> and
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*Y<rsup|2>=b>, then <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|H<rsub|n><around|(|X;a|)>*H<rsub|k><around|(|Y;b|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<space|0.17em><around|(|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|X*Y|]>|)><rsup|n>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|s*X-s<rsup|2>*a/2>\<mathe\><rsup|t*Y-t<rsup|2>*b/2>|]>=\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|s*X+t*Y|)><rsup|2>-<frac|1|2>*s<rsup|2>*a-<frac|1|2>*t<rsup|2>*b>=\<mathe\><rsup|s*t*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|X*Y|]>>>.
    Expand both sides in powers of <math|s,t> using <eqref|eq:hermite> (the
    exchange of sum and expectation is justified by Gaussian integrability)
    and compare coefficients.
  </proof>

  Fix an even mollifier <math|0\<leq\>\<eta\>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c><around|(|B<around|(|0,1|)>|)>>
  with <math|<big|int>\<eta\>=1>, <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<varepsilon\><rsup|-d>*\<eta\>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>/\<varepsilon\>|)>>
  (periodised on the torus), and let <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<varphi\>>,
  a smooth function. Then <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|y|)>|]>=G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
  with <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>=\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<eta\><rsub|\<delta\>>\<ast\>G>.
  Set <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<varepsilon\>><around|(|0|)>>
  and

  <\equation>
    :\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>H<rsub|n><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>,<space|2em>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>\<assign\><big|int>f<around|(|x|)>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x.
  </equation>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Wick powers><label|thm:wick> Let <math|n\<geq\>1> with
    <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d>, i.e. every <math|n> if
    <math|d\<leq\>2>, <math|n\<leq\>2> if <math|d=3>, <math|n=1> if
    <math|d\<geq\>4>. Then for every <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
    <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>\<assign\>lim<rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0>:\<varphi\><rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>:<around|(|f|)>>
    exists in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>, is independent of
    <math|\<eta\>>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wick-cov>\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>:\<varphi\><rsup|k>:<around|(|g|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<big|iint>f<around|(|x|)>*G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>*g<around|(|y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>y.
    </equation>

    If instead <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> and
    <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>, then <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: a uniform bound.> Let <math|K<around|(|z|)>=1+log<rsub|+><around|(|1/<around|\||z|\|>|)>>
    if <math|d=2>, <math|K<around|(|z|)>=1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2-d>> if
    <math|d\<geq\>3>, <math|K\<equiv\>1> if <math|d=1>. Then
    <math|0\<less\>G\<leq\>C<rsub|0>*K>, and we claim
    <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>\<leq\>C*K> uniformly in
    <math|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>. Let <math|r=max
    <around|(|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>|)>>. Then
    <math|<math-up|supp><around|(|\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<eta\><rsub|\<delta\>>|)>\<subset\>B<around|(|0,2*r|)>>,
    <math|<big|int>\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<eta\><rsub|\<delta\>>=1>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<eta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<ast\>\<eta\><rsub|\<delta\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leq\>r<rsup|-d><around|\<\|\|\>|\<eta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    If <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<geq\>4*r>, then
    <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>><around|(|z|)>\<leq\>sup<rsub|<around|\||w|\|>\<geq\><around|\||z|\|>/2>
    G<around|(|w|)>\<leq\>C<rsub|0>*K*<around|(|z/2|)>\<leq\>C*K<around|(|z|)>>.
    If <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>4*r>, then

    <\equation*>
      G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>><around|(|z|)>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<eta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-d>*<big|int><rsub|B<around|(|0,6*r|)>>G\<leq\>C*<frac|1|<around|\||B<around|(|0,6*r|)>|\|>>*<big|int><rsub|B<around|(|0,6*r|)>>K\<leq\>C*K<around|(|r|)>\<leq\>C<rprime|'>*K<around|(|z|)>.
    </equation*>

    <em|Step 2: convergence.> By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:hermite> and
    Fubini,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|k>:<around|(|g|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<big|iint>f<around|(|x|)>*g<around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>x<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>y.
    </equation*>

    As <math|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>\<to\>0>,
    <math|G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>><around|(|z|)>\<to\>G<around|(|z|)>>
    for <math|z\<neq\>0>. Moreover <math|K<rsup|n>\<in\>L<rsup|1><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>
    iff <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d>, and <math|K> is bounded on
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||z|\|>\<geq\>1|}>>. Since
    <math|f,g\<in\>L<rsup|1>\<cap\>L<rsup|\<infty\>>>, dominated convergence
    gives <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[||(>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>-:\<varphi\><rsub|\<delta\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>)<rsup|2>]=n!<big|iint>f*f*<space|0.17em><around|(|G<rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<varepsilon\>>-2*G<rsup|n><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>+G<rsup|n><rsub|\<delta\>,\<delta\>>|)>\<to\>0>:
    the sequence is Cauchy in <math|L<rsup|2>>. The limit covariance is
    <eqref|eq:wick-cov>. Running the same computation with two different
    mollifiers shows independence of <math|\<eta\>>. <em|Step 3: divergence.>
    If <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d>, then
    <math|G<rsup|n>\<geq\>c<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-n*<around|(|d-2|)>>> is not
    locally integrable, and Fatou's lemma gives <math|liminf
    \<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<geq\>n!<big|iint>f*f*G<rsup|n>=\<infty\>>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    (i) The subtraction of <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>\<infty\>> is
    the simplest instance of renormalisation:
    <math|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)><rsup|n>> itself has
    no limit when <math|d\<geq\>2>. (ii) The variables
    <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>> belong to the <math|n>-th
    Wiener chaos, and <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>\<simeq\><big|oplus><rsub|n>\<cal-H\><rsub|n>>
    is the symmetric Fock space over <math|H<rsup|-1><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>><nbsp><cite|Jan97>.
    By Nelson's hypercontractivity, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leq\><around|(|p-1|)><rsup|n/2><around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>>>
    for <math|X\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|n>>, so the convergence holds in every
    <math|L<rsup|p>>. A Kolmogorov-type argument gives convergence in
    <math|C<rsup|-\<kappa\>>> for <math|d=2>. (iii) In <math|d=2> on
    <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, the interaction
    <math|<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:\<mathd\>x> lies in every
    <math|L<rsup|p><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>, and Nelson's estimate gives
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>><nbsp><cite|Nel66|Sim74|GJ87>.
    This defines the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> measure on the torus.
    (iv) Other local operators: derivatives
    <math|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*\<varphi\>>, and in <math|d=2> the Wick
    exponentials <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\>>>: for
    <math|\<beta\><rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>> (in <math|L<rsup|2>>,
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:wickexp>), relevant for the sine-Gordon
    model, and the real exponentials <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<alpha\>*\<varphi\>>>:
    of the Høegh-Krohn model.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|Beyond <math|L<rsup|2>>: no random distribution.> When
  <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> the divergence in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:wick> is not an artefact of the
  <math|L<rsup|2>> norm: no other topology, and no other choice of
  probability space, can rescue the limit. Albeverio and
  Liang<nbsp><cite|AL07> showed this for the Wick square in <math|d=4>, and
  remarked that the argument extends to <math|d\<geq\>5>:
  <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|2>:<around|(|f|)>> is well defined as a Hida
  distribution, i.e. as a generalised functional of white noise, but for
  <math|f\<gtr\>0> the characteristic functions of its Fourier-cutoff
  approximations do not converge to a characteristic function (the natural
  candidate vanishes), so it is not a random variable. For <math|d\<leq\>3>,
  instead, it is a random variable, and an infinitely divisible one. The
  following argument is independent and covers all Wick powers.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:no-wick>Let <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> and
    <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>. Then the family
    <math|(:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>)<rsub|\<varepsilon\>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>>
    is not tight. In particular there is no random distribution
    <math|\<Psi\>>, on any probability space, such that
    <math|:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>><rsup|n>:\<to\>\<Psi\>> in
    law in <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>> along some sequence
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|n>>
    and <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>=\<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>>,
    so that <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>\<infty\>> by
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:wick>. The <em|Paley\UZygmund
    inequality><nbsp><cite|PZ32> states that for <math|Z\<geq\>0> with
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*Z<rsup|2>\<less\>\<infty\>> and
    <math|\<theta\>\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-P\>*<around|(|Z\<gtr\>\<theta\>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*Z|)>\<geq\><around|(|1-\<theta\>|)><rsup|2>*<frac|<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*Z|)><rsup|2>|\<bbb-E\>*Z<rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    (Proof: <math|\<bbb-E\>*Z\<leq\>\<theta\>*\<bbb-E\>*Z+\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Z*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|Z\<gtr\>\<theta\>*\<bbb-E\>*Z>|]>\<leq\>\<theta\>*\<bbb-E\>*Z+<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*Z<rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>*\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|Z\<gtr\>\<theta\>*\<bbb-E\>*Z|)><rsup|1/2>>
    by Cauchy\USchwarz.) Apply it to <math|Z=X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>>
    with <math|\<theta\>=<frac|1|4>>. Hypercontractivity with <math|p=4>
    gives <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|4>\<leq\>9<rsup|n>*\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|4>>,
    so

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-P\>*<around*|(|<around|\||X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\|>\<gtr\><tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>\<geq\><tfrac|9|16><space|0.17em>9<rsup|-n>=:c<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0.
    </equation*>

    Given <math|R\<gtr\>0>, for <math|\<varepsilon\>> small enough
    <math|<frac|1|2>*\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<gtr\>R> and hence
    <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|\|>\<gtr\>R|)>\<geq\>c<rsub|n>>:
    the family is not tight. If <math|:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>><rsup|n>:\<to\>\<Psi\>>
    in law in <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>>, then
    <math|X<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>\<to\>\<Psi\><around|(|f|)>> in law,
    and a sequence converging in law is tight, a contradiction.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The law of a bounded chaos is controlled by its <math|L<rsup|2>>
    norm><label|rem:chaos-norms>Let <math|X> be in the bounded chaos
    <math|<big|oplus><rsub|k\<leq\>n>\<cal-H\><rsub|k>>, e.g. a polynomial of
    degree <math|\<leq\>n> in finitely many Gaussian variables. Three
    inequalities control its law in terms of
    <math|\<sigma\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>>> alone, with
    constants that depend only on <math|n>:

    <\itemize>
      <item><em|upper tails> (hypercontractivity<nbsp><cite|Jan97>):
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|X|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leq\><around|(|p-1|)><rsup|n/2>*\<sigma\>>,
      hence <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X|\|>\<gtr\>t*\<sigma\>|)>\<leq\>exp
      <around|(|-c*<space|0.17em>t<rsup|2/n>|)>> for
      <math|t\<geq\>t<rsub|0>>;

      <item><em|no escape to zero> (Paley\UZygmund):
      <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X|\|>\<gtr\><frac|1|2>*\<sigma\>|)>\<geq\>c<rsub|n>\<gtr\>0>;

      <item><em|anti-concentration> (Carbery\UWright<nbsp><cite|CW01>):
      <math|\<bbb-P\>*<around|(|<around|\||X|\|>\<leq\>t*\<sigma\>|)>\<leq\>C*<space|0.17em>n*<space|0.17em>t<rsup|1/n>>
      for <math|t\<gtr\>0>.
    </itemize>

    Consequently, for sequences in a fixed bounded chaos, tightness is
    equivalent to boundedness in <math|L<rsup|2>>, and convergence in
    probability is equivalent to convergence in <math|L<rsup|2>> (and in
    every <math|L<rsup|p>>): apply the Paley\UZygmund bound to
    <math|X<rsub|k>-X<rsub|l>>. So for Wick powers there is a sharp
    dichotomy: either convergence in <math|L<rsup|2>>, or not even tightness.
    Nontrivial limits of the divergent objects require a change of
    normalisation: <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|-1>:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|n>>:
    is bounded in <math|L<rsup|2>>, and typically converges in law to a
    <em|Gaussian> white noise, which retains no information on the
    <math|n>-th power. The fourth moment theorem<nbsp><cite|NP05>
    characterises such Gaussian limits within a fixed chaos, and a central
    limit theorem of this kind for the lattice <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: in
    <math|d\<geq\>3> is proved in<nbsp><cite|AZ96>. In particular, in
    <math|d\<geq\>4> no Wick power with <math|n\<geq\>2> exists as a random
    distribution, so under the GFF the interaction
    <math|<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: cannot even be written down.

    <em|Acknowledgement.> I thank Ilya Chevyrev and Emilio Ferrucci for
    discussions clarifying this point.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Gaussian integration by parts and the Dyson\USchwinger
  equation>

  Let <math|\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>> be the space of cylinder
  functions <math|F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
  with <math|g\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>>
  and <math|f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>. For
  <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> define

  <\equation>
    D<rsub|h>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\><frac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>t>*F*<around|(|\<varphi\>+t*h|)><mid|\|><rsub|t=0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,h|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|D*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>,h|\<rangle\>>,
  </equation>

  with <math|D*F=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>*f<rsub|i>>,
  formally <math|D*F<around|(|x|)>=\<delta\>*F/\<delta\>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Gaussian integration by parts><label|lem:gibp> If
    <math|<around|(|Y,X<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,X<rsub|n>|)>> is a centred Gaussian
    vector and <math|g\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>>,
    then <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*g<around|(|X|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*X<rsub|i>|]>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|X|)>|]>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For <math|g=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,\<cdot\>|\<rangle\>>>>:
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|Y\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>=-\<mathi\><frac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>\<theta\>>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<theta\>*Y+\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>\|<rsub|\<theta\>=0>=\<mathi\><big|sum><rsub|i>u<rsub|i>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|Y*X<rsub|i>|]>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\><around|\<langle\>|u,X|\<rangle\>>>|]>>,
    which is the claim. The general case follows by Fourier inversion for
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>|)>> and then by
    approximation.
  </proof>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Dyson\USchwinger equation for the GFF><label|prop:DS> For all
    <math|F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>> and
    <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>|]>,<space|2em><text|formally><space|1em>\<bbb-E\><around*|[|<frac|\<delta\>*F|\<delta\>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>>|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|]>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Under <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, <math|<around|(|\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
    is centred Gaussian with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|i>|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|A*h,C*f<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|h,f<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:gibp>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|DS <math|=> Euclidean field equation><label|rem:DS> (i) Choosing
    <math|F=\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>>
    (allowed by approximation, since all moments are finite) gives the
    hierarchy for the Schwinger functions
    <math|S<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<varphi\><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<varphi\><around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|]>>:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS-hierarchy><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|x<rsub|1>>|)>*S<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j=2><rsup|n>\<delta\>*<around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|n-2><around|(|x<rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,<wide|x<rsub|j>|^>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|n>|)>,
    </equation>

    which together with <math|S<rsub|0>=1>, <math|S<rsub|1>=0> determines all
    moments (Wick's theorem, Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:wick-thm>). Compare
    with <hlink|(1.5)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:KG>: in imaginary time,
    <math|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>> becomes <math|-\<Delta\>+m<rsup|2>>, and the
    contact terms on the right come from the CCR, i.e. from time ordering.
    (ii) For a formal Gibbs measure <math|\<mu\><rsub|V>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsub|m>>
    the same computation gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS-V>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around*|[|F*<space|0.17em><around*|(|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|)><around|(|h|)>|]>,
    </equation>

    the Euclidean counterpart of the nonlinear field equation
    <hlink|(1.9)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:NLKG> (here
    <math|V=\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>>, so the <math|\<lambda\>> of
    <hlink|(1.9)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:NLKG> equals <math|4*\<lambda\>> here).
    Here <math|V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> must be a well-defined
    local operator: for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>,
    <math|V<rprime|'>=4*\<lambda\>:\<varphi\><rsup|3>>:, and the Euclidean
    field equation for <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> measures was
    proved by Dimock and Glimm<nbsp><cite|DG74>; see also the Dirichlet form
    approach of Albeverio and Röckner<nbsp><cite|AR91>. For
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the renormalised (relativistic) field
    equation, and with it the DS equations, were first derived by Feldman and
    R¡czka<nbsp><cite|FR77>, using the constructions of Glimm, Jaffe, Feldman
    and Osterwalder. The renormalised cubic term is not a random variable
    under the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> measure<nbsp><cite|ALZ06>; a
    description of it as a random distribution, and a proof of
    <eqref|eq:DS-V> for all <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0> in infinite volume, are
    given in<nbsp><cite|GH21>.

    (iii) <em|Point splitting and the operator product expansion.> The
    nonlinear term in <eqref|eq:DS-V> is a product of distributions at the
    same point, and the natural way to define it is by point splitting:
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|3><around|(|x|)>> is the regular part of
    <math|\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>>
    as <math|y<rsub|i>\<to\>x>. Under the GFF, the Wick decomposition of
    products (the multi-point version of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wick>,
    with <math|:\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>>:
    the product minus all its contractions) gives

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>>|<cell|=:\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>:>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+G*<around|(|y<rsub|1>-y<rsub|2>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|3>|)>+G*<around|(|y<rsub|1>-y<rsub|3>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|2>|)>+G*<around|(|y<rsub|2>-y<rsub|3>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|y<rsub|1>|)>,>>>>
    </align*>

    and the first term tends to <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|3>:<around|(|x|)>>.
    This is the beginning of an <em|operator product expansion> (OPE): a
    product of fields at nearby points is a sum of local operators at
    <math|x> with coefficients that are singular as the points merge, and the
    composite operator is what remains after the singular terms are
    subtracted. With a mollifier the same subtraction reads
    <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|3>\<assign\>lim<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|3>-3*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>,
    <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<bbb-E\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|0|)><rsup|2>>.
    For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> in finite volume the measure is
    absolutely continuous with respect to the GFF and the Gaussian
    subtraction suffices. In an interacting theory, however, the OPE
    coefficients are those of the interacting measure. For
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the cube needs an additional subtraction
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
    with <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> of order <math|\<lambda\>*log
    <around|(|1/\<varepsilon\>|)>>, so that
    <math|4*\<lambda\>*\<delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<sim\>\<lambda\><rsup|2>*log
    <around|(|1/\<varepsilon\>|)>> is the second-order (sunset) mass
    counterterm, i.e. the counterterm of the action reappears as an OPE
    coefficient of the interacting theory. Composite operators defined by the
    OPE are Wilson's short-distance expansion<nbsp><cite|Wil69> and
    Zimmermann's normal products<nbsp><cite|Zim73>, in terms of which the
    field equation of perturbative <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>> reads
    <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>=-4*\<lambda\>*N<around|[|\<varphi\><rsup|3>|]>>.
    Constructively, local products and field equations for
    <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> were obtained by point splitting
    by Schrader<nbsp><cite|Sch74>; the renormalised cube of<nbsp><cite|GH21>
    is approximated in the spirit of the OPE; convergence of the OPE in
    perturbation theory is proved in<nbsp><cite|HK12>. Lecture 3 describes
    the OPE at the level of stochastic observables, where the singular
    coefficients are explicit at every scale.
  </remark>

  The DS equation is not only satisfied by the GFF; it characterises it.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|DS characterises the GFF><label|thm:DS-unique> Let <math|\<nu\>>
    be a probability measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
    such that <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>
    for all <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:DS-nu>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|D<rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|F*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>|]>*<space|2em><text|for
      all >F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>,h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>.
    </equation>

    Then <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    Fix a real <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> and let
    <math|h=C*f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>, so that <math|A*h=f>.
    For <math|s\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> apply <eqref|eq:DS-nu> to the real and
    imaginary parts of <math|F<rsub|s><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>s*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>>,
    which belong to <math|\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>, using
    <math|D<rsub|h>*F<rsub|s>=\<mathi\>s*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>*F<rsub|s>>.
    With <math|\<chi\><around|(|s|)>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>><around|[|F<rsub|s>|]>>
    this gives

    <\equation*>
      \<mathi\>s*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>\<chi\><around|(|s|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>><around*|[|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>s*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>|]>=-\<mathi\>\<chi\><rprime|'><around|(|s|)>,
    </equation*>

    where we used <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>
    to differentiate under the expectation. Hence
    <math|\<chi\><rprime|'><around|(|s|)>=-s*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>*\<chi\><around|(|s|)>>
    with <math|\<chi\><around|(|0|)>=1>, so
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|s|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|s<rsup|2>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>.
    Taking <math|s=1>: <math|<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>\<mathd\>\<nu\>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    for every <math|f>. Since <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>u<rsub|j>*\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|j>|)>=\<varphi\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>u<rsub|j>*f<rsub|j>|)>>,
    this determines the characteristic function of every vector
    <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|k>|)>|)>>,
    hence all finite-dimensional distributions, hence <math|\<nu\>> on the
    cylinder <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra. So <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    The integrability assumption is the minimal one needed for
    <eqref|eq:DS-nu> to make sense, since <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*h|)>>
    ranges over all <math|\<varphi\><around|(|g|)>>,
    <math|g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>. Only test functions
    <math|F=\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>s*\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>> were used:
    DS turns into a first-order linear ODE for the characteristic function.
    For <math|m=0> on the torus no probability measure solves
    <eqref|eq:DS-nu> (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:massless>). For interacting
    theories, <eqref|eq:DS-V> is a singular nonlinear relation, and
    uniqueness of its solutions is a hard and largely open problem. In finite
    dimensions it is easy (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:DS-finite>). For a
    result in this direction for the exponential interaction in <math|d=2>
    see<nbsp><cite|DGT22>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The GFF as stationary marginal of parabolic stochastic
  quantisation>

  <paragraph|Langevin dynamics.> If <math|S:\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>>
  is smooth with <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<in\>L<rsup|1>>, the diffusion
  <math|\<mathd\>X<rsub|t>=-\<nabla\>*S<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|t>>
  has generator <math|\<cal-L\>=\<Delta\>-\<nabla\>*S\<cdot\>\<nabla\>>, and
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<ast\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>=\<nabla\>\<cdot\><around|(|\<nabla\>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>+\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<nabla\>*S|)>=0>:
  the Gibbs measure <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-S<around|(|x|)>>\<mathd\>x/Z> is
  invariant. Parisi and Wu<nbsp><cite|PW81> proposed to apply this to the
  Euclidean action, introducing a fictitious \Pstochastic time\Q <math|t>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:SQE>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\>=-<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<Phi\>-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<space|2em><text|on
    >\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
  </equation>

  with <math|\<xi\>> space-time white noise. This is <em|(parabolic)
  stochastic quantisation>. For <math|V=0> it is the stochastic heat
  equation, an infinite-dimensional Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process.

  <paragraph|History.> Parisi and Wu<nbsp><cite|PW81> introduced stochastic
  quantisation to do perturbation theory for gauge theories without gauge
  fixing. The physics literature of the 1980s is reviewed by Damgaard and
  Hüffel<nbsp><cite|DH87>, whose reprint volume<nbsp><cite|DH88> collects the
  original papers. Jona-Lasinio and Mitter<nbsp><cite|JLM85> gave the first
  mathematical construction of the dynamics for
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> on the torus, where the
  Wick-ordered interaction makes <eqref|eq:SQE> a singular SPDE. They used
  Girsanov's theorem for a variant of <eqref|eq:SQE> in which the drift is
  smoothed by <math|<around|(|1-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>> and the
  noise has covariance <math|<around|(|1-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>>
  (a slightly coloured noise), which still has the
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> measure as invariant measure. A
  recent review of the mathematical theory is<nbsp><cite|Gub25>.

  Space-time white noise on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> is a centred
  Gaussian family <math|<around|(|\<xi\><around|(|u|)>|)><rsub|u\<in\>L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>>
  with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<xi\><around|(|u|)>*\<xi\><around|(|v|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>,
  formally <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<xi\>*<around|(|t,x|)>*\<xi\>*<around|(|s,y|)>|]>=\<delta\>*<around|(|t-s|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>.
  Let <math|W<rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<xi\>*<around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|[|0,t|]>>\<otimes\>h|)>>,
  <math|t\<geq\>0>, a cylindrical Brownian motion. For a (weak) solution
  <math|\<Phi\>> of <eqref|eq:SQE> with <math|V=0> and
  <math|F=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>,
  Itô's formula shows that <math|F<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>\<cal-L\>*F<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|r>|)>\<mathd\>r>
  is a martingale, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:generator>\<cal-L\>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|DS <math|\<Rightarrow\>> invariance><label|prop:inf-inv> For
    <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>:
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|G*<space|0.17em>\<cal-L\>*F|]>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>><around|[|<around|\<langle\>|D*G,D*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>|]>>.
    In particular <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*F|]>=0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We may assume <math|F=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>>
    and <math|G=k*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>>
    with the same <math|f<rsub|i>>. Apply <eqref|eq:DS> to the cylinder
    function <math|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g> with
    <math|h=f<rsub|i>> and sum over <math|i>:
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|j>*k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g+k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g|]>>.
    Insert this into <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|G*\<cal-L\>*F|]>>.
  </proof>

  So the static characterisation (DS) and the dynamic one (invariance under
  the Langevin flow) are two faces of the same integration by parts formula.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Parabolic stochastic quantisation of the GFF><label|thm:parabolic>
    Let <math|\<xi\>> be space-time white noise on
    <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>. For <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:stat-sol>\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>\<assign\><sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around|(|K<rsub|t,f>|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|t,f><around|(|r,x|)>\<assign\><with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|{|r\<less\>t|}>><around*|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>f|)><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation>

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><math|\<Phi\>> is a centred Gaussian process with
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A>C*g|\<rangle\>>>.
      In particular <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>
      is stationary and, for every <math|t>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> has a
      version with values in <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
      whose law is <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>.

      <item><math|\<Phi\>> is a mild and weak solution of <eqref|eq:SQE> with
      <math|V=0>: for <math|s\<less\>t> and
      <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,

      <\gather*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-s|)>*A>h|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>h|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|h|)>-<big|int><rsub|s><rsup|t>\<Phi\><rsub|r>*<around|(|A*h|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>r+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>>\<otimes\>h|)>.>>>>
      </gather*>

      <item>Let <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>> be an
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued random variable
      independent of <math|\<xi\>>, and let <math|\<Psi\>> be the mild
      solution started from <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>>,

      <\equation*>
        \<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>=\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|0,t|)>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>f|)>,<space|2em>t\<geq\>0.
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>|]>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
      as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> for every
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>. Moreover
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> is invariant (<math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>>
      implies <math|\<Psi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>> for all <math|t>),
      and it is the unique invariant probability measure of the dynamics.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (a) Since <math|A> is self-adjoint and commutes with
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-u*A>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|t,f>,K<rsub|s,g>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|-\<infty\>><rsup|t\<wedge\>s><around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t+s-2*r|)>*A>g|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>r=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A><around|(|2*A|)><rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|K<rsub|t,f>\<in\>L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>,
    and multiplying by <math|2> gives the covariance. Stationarity follows.
    For fixed <math|t>, <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>> is
    linear and <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)><rsup|2>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>\<leq\>m<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>><rsup|2>>,
    so it is continuous from <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> to
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-P\>|)>>. By the regularisation theorem for
    random linear functionals on nuclear spaces (Itô\UNawata, Mitoma) it has
    an <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued version, which
    is centred Gaussian with covariance <math|C>, i.e. has law
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>. On the torus one can see this directly: the
    coordinates <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|e<rsub|n>|)>> are independent
    stationary OU processes <math|\<mathd\>X=-\<lambda\><rsub|n>*X\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2>\<mathd\>B>
    with variance <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1>>, and <eqref|eq:series>
    applies.

    (b) The mild formula is the identity <math|K<rsub|t,h>=K<rsub|s,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-s|)>*A>h>+<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>><around|(|r|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>h>
    together with the linearity of <math|\<xi\>>. The weak formulation
    follows from the mild one by a stochastic Fubini argument
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:weak>).

    (c) Since <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>> is independent of <math|\<xi\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-2*t*A>|)>*C*f|\<rangle\>>>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f\<to\>0> in
    <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>\<to\>0> a.s.
    because <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>.
    By dominated convergence the first factor tends to <math|1>, while the
    second tends to <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>.
    If <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>>, the first factor equals
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-2*t*A>C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    and the product is exactly <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>,
    which proves invariance. If <math|\<nu\>> is invariant, start from
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<nu\>>: then
    <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<nu\>> for all <math|t>,
    so the characteristic functional of <math|\<nu\>> equals that of
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, and <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|she_relaxation.pdf>|0.95par|||><label|fig:she>>|Parabolic
  stochastic quantisation of the GFF on a circle of length <math|8>,
  <math|m=1>, started from <math|\<Phi\><rsub|0>=0>. Left: a sample of the
  solution. Right: each Fourier mode is an Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process, whose
  variance relaxes to <math|\<lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>> at rate
  <math|2*\<lambda\><rsub|k>> (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(c)).
  Dots: Monte Carlo estimates with <math|4000> samples.>

  <\remark>
    As a Gaussian field on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>, the stationary
    solution has covariance <math|2*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>=2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    and its equal-time slice has covariance
    <math|2*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<frac|\<mathd\>\<theta\>|2*\<pi\>>=A<rsup|-1>>.
    The stochastic time <math|t> is unrelated to the Euclidean time
    <math|\<tau\>>. For interacting theories, <eqref|eq:SQE> is a singular
    SPDE. It was solved by Da Prato and Debussche for
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>><nbsp><cite|DPD03> (see
    also<nbsp><cite|JLM85|AR91>), and for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>
    using regularity structures and paracontrolled
    calculus<nbsp><cite|Hai14|GIP15|CC18>. Global-in-time
    bounds<nbsp><cite|MW17|GH19> then allow constructing the Euclidean
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> theory as the invariant measure of the
    dynamics<nbsp><cite|GH21>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The GFF as one-point marginal of elliptic stochastic
  quantisation>

  Parisi and Sourlas<nbsp><cite|PS79> discovered that the elliptic SPDE in
  <math|d+2> dimensions

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ESQ><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>|)>*\<Psi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Psi\>|)>=\<zeta\>,<space|2em>\<zeta\>*<text|white
    noise on >\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
  </equation>

  is related to the <math|d>-dimensional Gibbs measure: formally, the law of
  <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,\<cdot\>|)>>, <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>, is
  <math|\<propto\>exp <around*|(|-4*\<pi\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,A*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>|)>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>
  (<em|dimensional reduction>). The underlying mechanism is a hidden
  supersymmetry<nbsp><cite|PS79|KLP84>. In<nbsp><cite|KLP84> the dimensional
  reduction of the supersymmetric representation is proved rigorously, but
  not the supersymmetric representation of the solution of the SPDE; the full
  nonlinear statement was proved for <math|d=0> in<nbsp><cite|ADG20> and for
  some two-dimensional models (polynomial interactions on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, exponential interactions on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>) in<nbsp><cite|ADG21>; elliptic
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> equations in <math|4> and <math|5> dimensions are
  solved in<nbsp><cite|GH19>. For <math|V=0> the statement is elementary, and
  it explains the factor <math|4*\<pi\>>. We normalise the noise so that the
  slice is exactly <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Elliptic stochastic quantisation of the GFF><label|thm:elliptic>
    Let <math|\<zeta\>> be white noise on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> (an
    <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    Gaussian variable with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<zeta\><around|(|u|)>*\<zeta\><around|(|v|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>)
    and <math|<math-ss|L>\<assign\>m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>=-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>+A>.

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item>The equation <math|<math-ss|L>\<Psi\>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\>>
      has a unique <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
      solution, <math|\<Psi\><around|(|u|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1>u|)>>.

      <item>For <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> the function
      <math|K<rsub|z,f>\<assign\><math-ss|L><rsup|-1><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|z>\<otimes\>f|)>>
      lies in <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>,
      and <math|z\<mapsto\>K<rsub|z,f>> is continuous. The Wiener integrals
      <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>\<assign\><sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|K<rsub|z,f>|)>>
      satisfy <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|v\<otimes\>f|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>v<around|(|z|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z>
      a.s. for <math|v\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>|)>>.

      <item>For all <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> and
      <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z,g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>>.
      Hence <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,\<cdot\>|)>> has an
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued version with
      law <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, and the field
      <math|(\<Psi\>(z,\<cdot\><around|)||)><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>>
      is stationary and isotropic in <math|z>.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (a) <math|<math-ss|L>> is a continuous bijection of
    <math|\<cal-S\>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>, so
    <math|\<Psi\><around|(|u|)>\<assign\><sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*\<zeta\><around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1>u|)>>
    defines an <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>>-valued variable with
    <math|\<Psi\><around|(|<math-ss|L>u|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*\<zeta\><around|(|u|)>>.
    If <math|D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'>> satisfies
    <math|D<around|(|<math-ss|L>u|)>=0> for all <math|u\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, then
    <math|D=0> since <math|<math-ss|L>> is onto: the solution is unique.

    (b), (c) Take the Fourier transform in <math|z> only, with measure
    <math|\<mathd\>q/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>. The transform of
    <math|K<rsub|z,f>> is the <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    function <math|q\<mapsto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>q\<cdot\>z><around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-1>*f>.
    By Plancherel, the spectral theorem for <math|A> and Tonelli,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|z,f>,K<rsub|z,g>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>*g|\<rangle\>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>=<around*|\<langle\>|f,<around*|[|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>|]>*g|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>>*<around|\<langle\>|f,A<rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation*>

    using the scalar identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:4pi><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<frac|1|<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|r<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>r|<around|(|r<rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>*a>,<space|2em>a\<gtr\>0.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|K<rsub|z,f>\<in\>L<rsup|2>>, and its continuity in
    <math|z> follows by dominated convergence. Since the transform of
    <math|<big|int>v<around|(|z|)>*K<rsub|z,f>\<mathd\>z> is
    <math|<wide|v|^><around|(|q|)>*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-1>*f>,
    the Bochner integral <math|<big|int>v<around|(|z|)>*K<rsub|z,f>\<mathd\>z>
    equals <math|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1><around|(|v\<otimes\>f|)>>. The Wiener
    integral <math|\<zeta\>> is an isometry
    <math|L<rsup|2>\<to\>L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-P\>|)>> and commutes with
    Bochner integrals, which gives (b). Multiplying by <math|4*\<pi\>> gives
    the covariance in (c). The <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    version is obtained as in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>.
    Stationarity and isotropy hold because
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z<rprime|'>,g|)>|]>=4*\<pi\>*<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>q\<cdot\><around|(|z-z<rprime|'>|)>><around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>*g|\<rangle\>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>>
    depends only on <math|<around|\||z-z<rprime|'>|\|>>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Slices of Gaussian fields>Both stochastic quantisations exhibit
    the GFF as a <em|slice> of a Gaussian field in higher dimension. For the
    field on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> with covariance
    <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>+A|)><rsup|-p>>, the
    slice <math|<around|{|0|}>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> has covariance
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>*A<rsup|k/2-p>> with
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|p-<frac|k|2>|)>/<around|(|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|k/2>*\<Gamma\><around|(|p|)>|)>>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:slices>). The GFF arises iff <math|p=1+k/2>:
    <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|2,2|)>> is elliptic SQ, while
    <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>> gives instead the time-zero
    field <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>> of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:RP>. When
    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, the elliptic field <math|\<Psi\>> is
    Euclidean invariant in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d+2>>, and its restriction to
    <em|every> codimension-two affine subspace is a GFF.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Outlook>

  In Lecture 3 we decompose the GFF along a scale parameter: the field at
  resolution <math|t> is a Gaussian martingale, whose terminal value is the
  GFF. Interactions are then introduced through the Polchinski equation for
  the effective potential, and through its probabilistic form, a
  forward\Ubackward stochastic differential equation for the field at
  increasing resolution. These tools can be used to construct
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> and <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>. On
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> the Wick-ordered interaction
  <math|\<lambda\>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:\<mathd\>x> suffices, and the
  measure satisfies the renormalised DS equation <eqref|eq:DS-V>. In
  <math|d=3> Wick ordering no longer suffices.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Green functions><label|ex:green> (a) Using
    <math|G<rsub|m>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsup|2>*t>p<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>,
    with <math|p<rsub|t>> the heat kernel, show that
    <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-d/2>*<around|(|m/<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|d/2-1>*K<rsub|d/2-1><around|(|m<around|\||x|\|>|)>>,
    and derive the asymptotics stated after
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:gff>. (b) Check
    <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-m<around|\||x|\|>>/<around|(|2*m|)>>
    for <math|d=1> and <math|G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|-m<around|\||x|\|>>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><around|\||x|\|>|)>>
    for <math|d=3>. (c) Show that on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> the
    difference <math|G-G<rsub|m>> is smooth near the diagonal.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Euclidean Källén\ULehmann and the unitarity
    bound><label|ex:KL-euclid> (a) Show that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<mathd\>\<Omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|M><around|(|<math-bf|p>|)>\|\<tau\>\|+\<mathi\><math-bf|p>\<cdot\><math-bf|x>>=G<rsub|M>*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and derive <eqref|eq:KL-euclid> from <hlink|(1.4)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:KL>.
    (b) Show that <math|G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>> is decreasing in <math|M>
    and, for <math|d\<geq\>2>, that <math|G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>/G<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>\<to\>1>
    as <math|x\<to\>0>. Prove the lower bound in
    Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:KL-euclid>(i). (c) Let <math|d\<geq\>3> and
    suppose <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|T><around|(|x|)>=<around|\||x|\|><rsup|-2*\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>>>
    (a scale invariant theory; the scaling dimension
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>> is not the Laplacian). Using
    <math|G<rsub|M><around|(|x|)>=M<rsup|d-2>*G<rsub|1>*<around|(|M*x|)>>,
    show that <math|\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>=c<space|0.17em><around|(|M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>-d/2>\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>>
    reproduces <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|T>> for some <math|c\<gtr\>0>, for
    <math|<frac|d-2|2>\<less\>\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>\<less\><frac|d|2>>,
    and that this is a locally finite measure iff
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>\<gtr\><frac|d-2|2>> (for
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>\<geq\><frac|d|2>>,
    <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|T>> is not locally integrable). Using uniqueness of
    the Stieltjes transform, deduce the unitarity bound
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<varphi\>>\<geq\><frac|d-2|2>>, with equality iff
    <math|\<rho\>\<propto\>\<delta\><rsub|0>>, the massless free field.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Regularity is sharp><label|ex:regularity> (a) For
    <math|a<rsub|n>\<geq\>0> and <math|<around|(|g<rsub|n>|)>> i.i.d.
    <math|N<around|(|0,1|)>>, show that <math|\<bbb-E\><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|n>a<rsub|n>*g<rsub|n><rsup|2>>=<big|prod><rsub|n><around|(|1+2*a<rsub|n>|)><rsup|-1/2>>.
    Deduce that <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>a<rsub|n>*g<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    a.s. iff <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>a<rsub|n>\<less\>\<infty\>>. (b) Conclude
    that <math|\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|H<rsup|-s><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>|)>=1>
    iff <math|s\<gtr\><frac|d|2>-1>. In particular
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m><around|(|L<rsup|2>|)>=0> for <math|d\<geq\>2>, and
    the Cameron\UMartin space <math|H<rsup|1>> is <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>-null
    in every dimension. (c<rsup|<math|\<star\>>>) Using hypercontractivity
    and a Kolmogorov argument, show that <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsup|1-d/2-\<kappa\>>>
    a.s. for every <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Hermite calculus and change of renormalisation>(a) Show that
    <math|H<rsub|n><around|(|a;c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<leq\>n/2><frac|n!|k!<space|0.17em><around|(|n-2*k|)>!>*<around*|(|-<frac|c|2>|)><rsup|k>*a<rsup|n-2*k>>,
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*H<rsub|n>=n*H<rsub|n-1>> and
    <math|a*H<rsub|n>=H<rsub|n+1>+n*c*H<rsub|n-1>>. (b) Show that
    <math|H<rsub|n><around|(|a+b;c+c<rprime|'>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k><binom|n|k>H<rsub|k><around|(|a;c|)>*H<rsub|n-k><around|(|b;c<rprime|'>|)>>.
    (c) Show that <math|H<rsub|n><around|(|a;c<rprime|'>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k><binom|n|2*k><around|(|2*k-1|)>!!<space|0.17em><around|(|c-c<rprime|'>|)><rsup|k>*H<rsub|n-2*k><around|(|a;c|)>>.
    Interpret this as the effect of changing the mollifier on
    <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>:: Wick powers are defined up to lower order
    Wick powers with <em|finite> coefficients.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Wick powers: details>(a) Show that
    <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|1|\<varepsilon\>>+O<around|(|1|)>> for <math|d=2> and
    <math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<asymp\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|2-d>> for
    <math|d\<geq\>3>. (b) In <math|d=3>, show that
    <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|3>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<asymp\>log
    <frac|1|\<varepsilon\>>> for <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>: the cube is
    borderline divergent. (c) In <math|d=4>, show that
    <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>:<around|(|f|)><rsup|2>]\<asymp\>log
    <frac|1|\<varepsilon\>>> as well.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Wick exponentials><label|ex:wickexp> In <math|d=2> let
    <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsup|2>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>/2>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|x|)>>>.
    Show that

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em><wide|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<delta\>>>:<around|(|y|)>|\<bar\>>|]>=\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsup|2>*G<rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<delta\>>*<around|(|x-y|)>>,
    </equation*>

    and deduce that <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<beta\>*\<varphi\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>:<around|(|f|)>>
    converges in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>> for every
    <math|f> if <math|\<beta\><rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>>, while its second
    moment diverges for <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0> if
    <math|\<beta\><rsup|2>\<geq\>4*\<pi\>>. What changes for the real
    exponential <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<alpha\>*\<varphi\>>>:? (Gaussian
    multiplicative chaos exists in <math|L<rsup|1>> for
    <math|\<alpha\><rsup|2>\<less\>8*\<pi\>>, see<nbsp><cite|BP25>.)
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Wick's theorem from DS><label|ex:wick-thm> (a) Complete the proof
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:gibp>. (b) Derive <eqref|eq:DS-hierarchy>
    from <eqref|eq:DS>, justifying the use of polynomial <math|F>. (c) Deduce
    Isserlis\UWick: <math|S<rsub|2*n+1>=0> and
    <math|S<rsub|2*n><around|(|x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|2*n>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|<text|pairings
    >P><big|prod><rsub|<around|{|i,j|}>\<in\>P>G*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|DS in finite dimensions><label|ex:DS-finite> Let
    <math|S<around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,Q*x|\<rangle\>>+V<around|(|x|)>>
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>>, with <math|Q\<gtr\>0> and <math|V> smooth
    with <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V>> bounded, and
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|V><around|(|\<mathd\>x|)>=Z<rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-S<around|(|x|)>>\<mathd\>x>.
    (a) Show that <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|V>>*<around|[|F*<around|\<langle\>|\<nabla\>*S,h|\<rangle\>>|]>>
    for <math|F\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|c>>. (b) Let <math|\<nu\>> be a
    probability measure with <math|\<nabla\>*S\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>
    satisfying the same identity. Show that <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|V>>.
    <em|Hint:> the identity says that the distribution
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|S>\<nu\>> has zero gradient. (c) Why does this
    argument fail in infinite dimensions? What replaces it in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:DS-unique>?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The massless case><label|ex:massless> (a) Show that for <math|m=0>
    on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>> no probability measure on
    <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>|)>> satisfies
    <eqref|eq:DS-nu> with <math|A=-\<Delta\>>. <em|Hint:> take <math|h=1>.
    (b) Show that the massless GFF exists on the quotient
    <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d>|)>/\<bbb-R\>> (test
    functions with <math|<big|int>f=0>), and characterise it by DS there. (c)
    On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>, show that
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>*f|\<rangle\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    iff <math|<wide|f|^><around|(|0|)>=0>: the infrared problem of the
    two-dimensional massless field.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Infinitesimal invariance characterises <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>>On
    <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>>, let <math|\<nu\>> be a probability
    measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>>
    with <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>
    and <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*F|]>=0> for all
    <math|F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>. Show that
    <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>. <em|Hint:> for
    <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|n\<leq\>N>a<rsub|n>*e<rsub|n>> and
    <math|\<psi\><around|(|a|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>>,
    show <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>\<lambda\><rsub|n>*a<rsub|n>*\<partial\><rsub|a<rsub|n>>*\<psi\>=-<around|\||a|\|><rsup|2>*\<psi\>>,
    and differentiate <math|s\<mapsto\>\<psi\><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|s*D>a|)>*exp
    <around|(|<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|n>a<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<mathe\><rsup|2*\<lambda\><rsub|n>*s>/\<lambda\><rsub|n>|)>>,
    where <math|D=<math-up|diag><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|n>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Reversibility>Use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inf-inv> to
    show that <math|\<cal-L\>> is symmetric in
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>. Deduce, and check directly
    from the covariance in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(a), that
    the stationary solution is time reversible:
    <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|-t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> have the same
    law.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Mild implies weak><label|ex:weak> Prove the weak formulation in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(b). Show that, as a random
    distribution on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>, the stationary
    solution has covariance operator <math|2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Slices of Gaussian fields><label|ex:slices> Let <math|k\<geq\>1>,
    <math|p\<gtr\>k/2>, and let <math|\<Psi\>> be the centred Gaussian field
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> with covariance
    <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>+A|)><rsup|-p>>. (a)
    Show that <math|\<Psi\><around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>> is well defined with
    covariance <math|c<rsub|k,p>*A<rsup|k/2-p>>,
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|p-<frac|k|2>|)>/<around|(|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|k/2>*\<Gamma\><around|(|p|)>|)>>.
    (b) For <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>> and <math|\<cal-M\>=N>,
    show that the time-zero field of the GFF on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>N> is
    the ground state measure <math|\<Omega\><rsub|0><around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>
    of the free Hamiltonian, i.e. Gaussian with covariance
    <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>> (compare
    Exercise<nbsp><hlink|2.2|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:OU>). (c) Show that the
    restriction of the elliptic field of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:elliptic>
    to a codimension-<em|one> slice <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> has
    covariance proportional to <math|<around|(|-\<partial\><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-3/2>>,
    so it is not a GFF. (d) Show that <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|0,g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|\||z|\|>*A<rsup|-1/2>*K<rsub|1>*<around|(|<around|\||z|\|>*A<rsup|1/2>|)>*g|\<rangle\>>>
    in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:elliptic>, and recover
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>> as <math|z\<to\>0>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Lattice approximation><label|ex:lattice-gff> Let
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> be the centred Gaussian measure on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|<around|(|\<varepsilon\>*\<bbb-Z\>/L*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|d>>>
    with covariance <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
    (with respect to <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*<big|sum><rsub|u>>),
    embedded in <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>>
    via <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*<big|sum><rsub|u>\<varphi\><rsub|u>*f<around|(|u|)>>.
    Show that <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>\<mu\><rsub|m>> weakly.
    Relate <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> to
    <hlink|(2.12)|cqft_notes_L1.tm#eq:spin-system> with <math|V=0> (for
    <math|d=2>; in general <math|d> see Exercise<nbsp><hlink|2.3|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:lattice-action>).
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Computer experiments>(a) Sample the lattice GFF of
    Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:lattice-gff> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> with
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=1/N> (i.e. on <math|<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/N*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|2>>
    with mass <math|m/N> in lattice units) by FFT, for <math|N=64,256,1024>,
    and check that the empirical variance of <math|\<varphi\><rsub|u>> grows
    like <math|<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log N>. (b) Implement an explicit Euler
    scheme for the lattice version of <eqref|eq:SQE> with <math|V=0> (mind
    the stability constraint <math|\<Delta\>*t\<lesssim\>\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>>),
    and check that the variance of the <math|k>-th Fourier mode relaxes to
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>> at rate <math|2*\<lambda\><rsub|k>>,
    with <math|\<lambda\><rsub|k>> the eigenvalues of
    <math|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>. (c) Add
    <math|V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=4*\<lambda\>*<around|(|\<varphi\><rsup|3>-3*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>|)>>,
    i.e. <math|V=\<lambda\>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>:, and observe that the
    solutions have a nontrivial limit as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, while
    without the counterterm <math|-12*\<lambda\>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<varphi\>>
    they converge to zero<nbsp><cite|HRW12>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Two masses><label|ex:two-masses> In the setting of the example
    after Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:KL-euclid>: (a) Show that
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\>>> satisfies (OS0)\U(OS4), and that the theory
    described there has Schwinger functions equal to the moments of
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<rho\>>>. (b) Show that
    <math|<around|[|\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,<math-bf|x>|)>,\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,<math-bf|y>|)>|]>=\<mathi\><around|(|c<rsub|1><rsup|2>+c<rsub|2><rsup|2>|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|<math-bf|x>-<math-bf|y>|)>>,
    and that <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|t,\<cdot\>|)>> is not a linear
    function of <math|\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<varphi\>*<around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>>. (c) Verify
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|D<rsub|R*h>*F|]>=\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|F*\<varphi\>*<around|(|P*h|)>|]>>.
    (d) For a single spatial Fourier mode <math|<math-bf|p>> (on a torus,
    say, with real test functions <math|f*<around|(|\<tau\>,<math-bf|x>|)>=g<around|(|\<tau\>|)>*cos
    <around|(|<math-bf|p>\<cdot\><math-bf|x>|)>>), with
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i><rsup|2>=<around|\||<math-bf|p>|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsub|i><rsup|2>>,
    show that the time kernel of <math|P<rsup|-1>> is
    <math|<around*|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|1><around|\||\<tau\>|\|>>/2*\<omega\><rsub|1>-\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|2><around|\||\<tau\>|\|>>/2*\<omega\><rsub|2>|)>/<around|(|m<rsub|2><rsup|2>-m<rsub|1><rsup|2>|)>>,
    and find <math|f> supported in <math|<around|{|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|}>> with
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*f,P<rsup|-1>*f|\<rangle\>>\<less\>0>.
    <em|Hint:> choose <math|g> with <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|1>*\<tau\>>g<around|(|\<tau\>|)>\<mathd\>\<tau\>=0>.
    (e) Let <math|p> be a real polynomial of degree <math|\<geq\>2>, positive
    on <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>. Show that
    <math|1/p<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)>> is not of the form
    <math|<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    with <math|\<rho\>\<geq\>0>. <em|Hint:>
    <math|s*<big|int>\<rho\><around|(|\<mathd\>M<rsup|2>|)>*<around|(|s+M<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    increases to <math|\<rho\><around|(|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>|)>> as
    <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>> (monotone convergence), while
    <math|s/p<around|(|s|)>\<to\>0>.
  </exercise>

  <section*|References and further reading>

  <paragraph|Guide.> <em|Gaussian fields and Wick calculus:>
  <cite|GV64|Bog98|Jan97|She07|LSSW16|BP25>. <em|Nonexistence of Wick powers
  beyond <math|L<rsup|2>> and chaos inequalities:>
  <cite|AL07|PZ32|CW01|NP05|AZ96>. <em|Euclidean free field and Markov
  property:> <cite|Sym66|Sym69|Nel73a|Nel73b|GRS75|GJ87|Sim74|Gue06>.
  <em|Path and loop representations:> <cite|Sym69|BFS82|Dyn84|LW04|LJ10|LJ11|Lup16|Wer16|QW19|WP21>.
  <em|Stochastic quantisation:> reviews <cite|DH87|DH88|Gub25>; parabolic
  <cite|PW81|JLM85|AR91|DPD03|DPZ14>; singular SPDEs
  <cite|Hai14|GIP15|CC18|MW17|HRW12>; construction of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> via dynamics and variational methods
  <cite|GH21|BG20>; Euclidean field equations and DS equations for
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> <cite|DG74|AR91>, for
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> <cite|FR77|ALZ06|GH21>, and for the
  exponential model in <math|d=2> <cite|DGT22>; operator product expansion
  and composite operators <cite|Wil69|Zim73|Sch74|HK12>; elliptic stochastic
  quantisation and dimensional reduction <cite|PS79|KLP84|ADG20|ADG21|GH19>.

